          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.2 Reading and Evaluating Complex Expressions"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.339,
            0.848,
            0.413
          ],
          "content": "Sometimes, when an expression is not accompanied by a context, there can be more than one way of evaluating its value. In such cases, we need some tools and rules to specify how exactly the expression has to be evaluated. 2K6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.413,
            0.574,
            0.452
          ],
          "content": "To give an example with language, look at the following sentences:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.182,
            0.456,
            0.573,
            0.494
          ],
          "content": "(a) Sentence: “Shalini sat next to a friend with toys”."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.226,
            0.498,
            0.573,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": "Meaning: The friend has toys and Shalini sat next to her."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.593,
            0.402,
            0.767,
            0.543
          ],
          "content": "",
