        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.457,
            0.559,
            0.847,
            0.597
          ],
          "content": "(b) Sentence: “Shalini sat next to a friend, with toys”."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.499,
            0.602,
            0.848,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": "Meaning: Shalini has the toys and she sat with them next to her friend."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.693,
            0.847,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "This sentence without the punctuation could have been interpreted in two different ways. The appropriate use of a comma specifies how the sentence has to be understood."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.749,
            0.839,
            0.768
          ],
          "content": "Let us see an expression that can be evaluated in more than one way."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.777,
            0.848,
            0.836
          ],
          "content": "？ **Example 4:** Mallesh brought 30 marbles to the playground. Arun brought 5 bags of marbles with 4 marbles in each bag. How many marbles did Mallesh and Arun bring to the playground?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.839,
            0.831,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": "Mallesh summarized this by writing the mathematical expression —"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.447,
            0.864,
            0.551,
            0.882
          ],
          "content": "3 0 \\pm 5 \\times 4 .",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_23.jpg"
        },
